<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">label</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Adding Attributes</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            Attributes are the individual fields of an entity &mdash; its name, type, and constraints.
            They hold the actual values that each record stores.
        </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Add an attribute</h2>
    <ol class="help-steps">
        <li>
            <strong>Select an entity</strong> in the source list.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Open the main menu</strong> and choose <em>Add Attribute</em>.
            A new attribute is added to the selected entity.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Name it and pick a type</strong> in the Inspector &mdash; String, Integer, Boolean,
            Date, Decimal, Binary, and more. You can also choose one of your model's composite types.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Common options</h2>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Optional</strong> &mdash; whether the field may be left empty. Clear it to make the attribute required.</li>
        <li><strong>Transient</strong> &mdash; the value is computed at runtime and never written to the store, so it has no column.</li>
        <li><strong>Derived</strong> &mdash; the value comes from a derivation expression rather than being set directly.</li>
        <li><strong>Sensitive</strong> &mdash; marks data that should be handled carefully (for example, excluded where appropriate).</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Depending on the type, the <strong>Configuration</strong> section lets you set a default value,
        a permitted range or length, a validation pattern, and other type-specific rules.
    </p>

    <p>
        Attributes also support versioning fields, access controls, and roles. Use those sections when
        you need generated endpoints to expose a field only to specific users or scopes.
    </p>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">functions</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Derived attributes use an expression, not a predicate.</strong>
            The derivation is an expression that produces a value. For a derived boolean, use a form like
            <code>TERNARY(x == n, 1, 0)</code> rather than a comparison such as <code>x = n</code>.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Next steps</h2>
    <p>
        With attributes in place, connect this entity to others by
        <a class="help-link" href="#relationships" data-help-anchor="relationships">adding relationships</a>,
        or define reusable value shapes with
        <a class="help-link" href="#composite-types" data-help-anchor="composite-types">composite types</a>.
    </p>
</article>
